## Vec Owns Its Elements

A `Vec[T]` owns every `T` inside it. Moving the Vec moves all elements:

```xiom
fn consume_items(items: Vec[Int]) {
  io.println(items.len());
  // All elements dropped with Vec
}

fn main() -> Int {
  let numbers = Vec[Int].new();
  numbers.push(1);
  numbers.push(2);
  consume_items(numbers);  // Entire Vec + elements moved
  return 0;
}
```

### Borrowing for Iteration

```xiom
fn sum_items(items: &Vec[Int]) -> Int {
  let total = 0;
  for item in items {  // Iterates over &Int references
    total = total + item;
  }
  return total;
}
```

### Modifying Through &mut

```xiom
fn double_all(items: &mut Vec[Int]) {
  let i = 0;
  while i < items.len() {
    items[i] = items[i] * 2;
    i = i + 1;
  }
}

fn main() -> Int {
  var data = Vec[Int].new();
  data.push(1);
  data.push(2);
  data.push(3);

  io.println(sum_items(&data));  // Read — OK
  double_all(&mut data);          // Write — OK
  io.println(sum_items(&data));  // Read — OK
  return data.len() as Int;
}
```

### Vec Element Ownership

Individual elements follow the same rules. Accessing `vec[0]` through a borrow gives you a reference, not ownership.

